  "worked_solution": "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=\u27e8-3,2,1\u27e9.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+6t \\\\ y=3-5t \\\\ z=-2+4t\\end{aligned}\\,\u21d2\\,\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9=\u27e8-1,3,-2\u27e9+t\\text{\u00a0}{{\u27e86,-5,4\u27e9}_{\u23df}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=\u27e86,-5,4\u27e9.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=\u27e86,-5,4\u27e9.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e86,-5,4\u27e9 & =\u27e8-3,2,1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e86,-5,4\u27e9 \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e86,-5,4\u27e9 & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
